\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Block diagram tracing a five-stage operational architecture for autonomous robots, sequencing raw sensory input, feature state encoding, deep self-model kinematics processing, tracking control planning, and joint execution loops.}}{3}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Horizontal developmental timeline marking benchmark historical milestones in robotic self-modeling research from early 2006 symbolic explorations to contemporary real-time neural rendering architectures.}}{3}{figure.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Historical taxonomy of robot self-modeling paradigms: chronological evolution from symbolic approaches (2006) through behavioral learning, learned representations, visual estimation, to deployable systems (2026).}}{9}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces System workflow chart mapping continuous data collection loops from physical hardware to calibrate a simulation environment, detailing online deployment execution and residual error matching pathways.}}{16}{figure.caption.9}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces PyBullet simulation setup for the 4-DOF manipulator, including the robot model, target marker, and virtual camera frusta used for synthetic data generation.}}{26}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Lorenz System Trajectory generating smooth, chaotic workspace exploration. The attractor produces deterministic non-repeating paths that densely cover the robot's configuration space.}}{28}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces End-to-end data generation and preprocessing pipeline. RGB images from PyBullet are converted to grayscale and thresholded to produce binary silhouettes for training.}}{29}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Representative robot configurations from the dataset, demonstrating diverse poses including vertical extension, diagonal reach, and angled configurations.}}{30}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces FFKSM target versus render showing visual discrepancies. Volumetric rendering produces blurry depth predictions; errors concentrate at joint boundaries and link extremities where sampling density is insufficient.}}{37}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces FFKSM training dynamics: (left) MSE loss convergence over 150 epochs; (right) validation loss trajectory confirming generalization without overfitting.}}{37}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces K-3DGS render artifacts and spatial difference map. Isotropic spherical Gaussians create overlapping "blobby" artifacts that blur link boundaries.}}{42}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces K-3DGS training metrics showing loss convergence (top), FPS (middle), and step time (bottom). Inference speed stabilizes at 37 FPS after initial optimization.}}{42}{figure.caption.40}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces NeuroKin convolutional decoder architecture. Joint-angle inputs are encoded into a latent representation, spatially expanded, and decoded through transposed convolution layers into a predicted visual self-model.}}{47}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Stereoscopic training samples: robot silhouettes captured from two orthogonal camera viewpoints for multi-view depth reconstruction.}}{52}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces 3D workspace coverage: end-effector positions for 2000 training and test samples, demonstrating complete reachable workspace exploration.}}{53}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces NeuroKin training dynamics: (left) MSE loss convergence over 60 epochs; (right) PSNR reconstruction quality across validation set.}}{56}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Base NeuroKin qualitative validation results. Target silhouettes, NeuroKin renders, and difference maps show that direct decoding recovers link geometry with localized errors near joint boundaries and the end-effector.}}{59}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces NeuroKin-3D validation coordinates. Predicted $x$, $y$, and $z$ traces closely follow the ground-truth end-effector positions across the validation sequence (mean error 2.39 mm, $R^2 = 0.997$).}}{60}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces NeuroKin-3D validation diagnostics: Euclidean end-effector error, predicted versus target 3D trajectory, and per-joint mean absolute error.}}{61}{figure.caption.74}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Closed-loop control architecture: single-agent inverse kinematics loop with state initialization, NeuroKin-3D visual state estimation, Jacobian computation, DLS pseudoinverse solver, and velocity integration.}}{67}{figure.caption.75}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Nominal closed-loop control: end-effector error convergence trajectory from 100 independent reaching trials under perfect calibration.}}{69}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces 3D workspace trajectory during nominal reaching: end-effector path in Cartesian space from initial home configuration to target, achieved via closed-loop visual feedback.}}{70}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Individual joint error dynamics during nominal control: simultaneous convergence profiles (radians) across all four joints over 200 control steps.}}{71}{figure.caption.78}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Dynamic error recovery following encoder slip injected at Step 50. The baseline pure proprioceptive controller diverges catastrophically, while NeuroKin's visual estimates enable rapid re-convergence to the target.}}{74}{figure.caption.81}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Sequential swarm temporal stigmergy architecture: multi-agent feedback loop with shared factory state, per-agent policy networks, threshold-based recovery triggers, and success/failure outcome updates.}}{85}{figure.caption.91}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Sequential swarm performance summary: (left) success rate across three conditions (baseline pre-fault, baseline post-fault, NeuroKin post-fault); (right) mean steps-to-convergence for each condition.}}{88}{figure.caption.96}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Algorithm efficiency in sequential swarm: steps-to-convergence scatter plot across 100 stages under progressive fault injection, comparing baseline (blue) vs. NeuroKin (orange).}}{89}{figure.caption.97}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Pareto frontier illustrating the optimal trade-off between model accuracy (PSNR) and computational speed (FPS). NeuroKin occupies the upper-right region, achieving both superior quality and sub-millisecond latency. The red dashed line indicates the 1,000 FPS threshold for 1 kHz control loops.}}{96}{figure.caption.99}%
